{
	"nodes":[
		{"id":"core","type":"file","file":"qualitative_analysis/04_cross_case_analysis/core_phenomenon.md","x":-660,"y":-1080,"width":360,"height":140},
		{"id":"sampling","type":"file","file":"qualitative_analysis/04_cross_case_analysis/theoretical_sampling_plan.md","x":-2220,"y":-460,"width":360,"height":188},
		{"id":"next_decision","type":"text","text":"## Current sampling decision\n\nCan students from a large modelling course realistically be recruited?\n\nIf yes: prioritize 2-3 large-course students with contrasting participation/feedback patterns.\n\nIf no: move to the next feasible theoretical sample, such as repeated-exposure students, project-maintenance cases, or disengaged/missed-class students.","x":-2250,"y":-120,"width":420,"height":230},
		{"id":"cautions","type":"text","text":"## Methodological cautions\n\nS4-S6 are reconstructed transcripts.\nS2 speaker attribution may need verification.\nLecturer interpretations of student behaviour need student-side confirmation.\nTool-support proposals are requirements/design implications, not validated intervention effects.\nUse bounded category elaboration rather than claiming full saturation.","x":-2255,"y":280,"width":430,"height":220},
		{"id":"axial","type":"file","file":"qualitative_analysis/04_cross_case_analysis/axial_model.md","x":-1400,"y":-505,"width":360,"height":90},
		{"id":"categories","type":"file","file":"qualitative_analysis/04_cross_case_analysis/category_framework.md","x":-1220,"y":-815,"width":360,"height":130},
		{"id":"comparisons","type":"text","text":"## Cross-case comparisons\n\nThematic comparison notes test and refine claims across cases, including examples, feedback, domains, ambiguity, tool support, modelling value, workload, AI, and cross-family modelling.","x":-1070,"y":785,"width":420,"height":150},
		{"id":"responses","type":"text","text":"## Learner-response modes\n\n1. Defer to authority\n2. Explore and justify alternatives\n3. Negotiate collaboratively\n4. Avoid or abandon modelling\n\nFamiliar-pattern reuse cuts across all four.","x":-680,"y":-305,"width":380,"height":185},
		{"id":"outcomes","type":"text","text":"## Outcomes and value\n\nSupported modelling can produce independent judgement, ambiguity tolerance, communication, coordination, maintained artefacts, and perceived value.\n\nUnsupported modelling can produce dependence, compliance, model decay, or avoidance.","x":570,"y":50,"width":390,"height":190},
		{"id":"support","type":"text","text":"## Support mechanisms\n\nPedagogical support: examples, criteria, feedback, revision, critique.\n\nTechnical support: validation, explainable guidance, traceability, collaboration, AI critique with learner accountability.","x":-675,"y":110,"width":390,"height":205},
		{"id":"negative_cases","type":"text","text":"## Negative-case tests\n\nCurrent refinements: uncertainty can move stages; critique needs safety and substance; domain choice and continuous assessment are conditional; semantic tools have costs; formality should be purpose-proportionate.","x":-1070,"y":1111,"width":420,"height":170},
		{"id":"comp_value","type":"file","file":"qualitative_analysis/04_cross_case_analysis/comparison_modelling_value.md","x":0,"y":615,"width":330,"height":65},
